 and serious  illnesses and how does that change how  the federal government will be thinking  about  omicron and copa 19 as it becomes  endemic  okay well why don't we turn to dr  wilinski on both dr linsky first when  when americans should test  yeah so so simply put americans should  take a test when they have symptoms that  appear to be cova-19 so  fever cough sore throat respiratory  symptoms muscle aches  when they're exposed so five days after  they've been exposed to someone who has  cova-19 and certainly if you're going to  um gather with uh with family if you're  going to a gathering where people are  immunocompromised or aware they're  elderly or where you have um people who  might be unvaccinated or poorly  protected from the vaccine that might be  an opportunity you'd want to test and  then of course for tests to stay in  other protocols um with regard to this  study actually only compared omicron to  delta and not to other respiratory  viruses certainly other respiratory  viruses influenza for example vary the  severity varies by flu season so this  was not a direct comparison of um  omicron versus other respiratory viruses  but what i can say is we are while we're  in this omicron surge we are also  thinking about what this looks like with  um endemic when omicron or code itself  were to become endemic in the united  states following things such as severity  of cases hospital capacity as well thank  you  next question  please let's go to sabrina siddiki wall  street journal  thank you for taking the call um i  wanted to have asked a follow-up about  the school testing  expansion for these tests that you're  making available for k-12 schools can  you clarify would only public schools be  eligible to receive those tests or  private schools as well and can you  offer any other detail on where the  tests are coming from have you already  inked contracts with certain companies  or is that process still ongoing  well let me just step back and say that  you 